ok today we're going to learn about  infrared radiation and some of its uses  so to begin with it's important to know  that infrared is a part of the  electromagnetic spectrum it's it's just  between microwaves and visible light  just like all of the other parts of the  electromagnetic spectrum it's a type of  transverse wave that travels at 300  million meters per second in a vacuum  that's the speed of light so what we're  going to do is we're going to dive  straight in and learn about some of the  uses of infrared radiation so number one  is thermal imaging so whenever we think  about infrared we often think about heat  because that's that's how a lot of heat  is transferred so if we think about the  thermal imaging it works like this  because infrared radiation is given out  by every hot object the hotter the  object the more infrared radiation it  gives out so even though we can't see it  because the only parts of the  electromagnetic spectrum we can see is  the visible light spectrum even though  we can't see it if we're if anything is  a hot object it's giving out this  radiation now obviously our eyes can't  detect it but we can use equipment  called infrared infrared cameras that  can detect this infrared radiation in  order to create an image hence thermal  imaging creating an image using heat  now often police can use it to see  suspects that are trying to escape and  hide in the dark they often have  infrared cameras fitted on helicopters  if they're chasing suspects another use  is cooking so what I've got right here  is I've got a cross-section of a toaster  and here's some bread that's been put  into the toaster so what actually can  happen  with cooking is infrared radiation is  given out by the heating elements of the  toaster so all the way across the  heating elements were given out giving  out infrared radiation now what happens  in order for this to work food absorbs  the infrared radiation and as it does it  causes it to to cook so that is how  cooking works with infrared radiation  number three is heaters so again if you  have a heater you basically it emits  lots of infrared radiation that can then  travel or transmit throughout the room  it basically transfers lots of energy to  the thermal store of different objects  causing their temperature to increase  you do need to know though that if you  have too much of this infrared radiation  it is absorbed by the skin  and it can cause burns so infrared  radiation can be dangerous because it  can cause burns okay next up we have  remote controls so the first three kind  of demonstrates infrared radiation in  terms of heat or in terms of yet in  terms of heat but we can also use it to  transfer information so lots and lots of  remote controls use infrared radiation  in order to transfer information so when  you press the buttons on your remote it  Tran it emits infrared radiation which  can then be picked up by receiver within  let's say your television now the reason  why that it it works for this is  basically the distances need to be  fairly small and it must be in the line  of sight so you can't really turn on  off your TV for devices that are next  door for example now the final one we  need to know about the final use is  optical fibers  now optical fibers are thin glass or  plastic fibers that carry data for  example for telephones and computers  they travel they kind of transfer that  carry the data over long distances  now what happens is the infrared travels  along the wire along the optical fiber  using a process referred to as total  internal reflection and you can learn  more about that in another video so  essentially those are the five uses of  infrared radiation we need to know about  thermal imaging cooking heaters remote  controls and optical fibers and a  dangerous thing about infrared radiation  is our skin can absorb it so infrared  radiation can cause burns thank you
 

